      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-221701",
      ":question/problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n+2$ is odd for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}} \n$5n+2$ is {{field-4}}.\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n+2 & =5({{field-6}})+2 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}}. \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false."
    },
